The attached image illustrates the dendrimer-like structure of the phytoglycogen nanoparticles, referred to as Nano-11 in the PDF. These nanoparticles are characterized by a dense, highly branched internal structure radiating from a central core, which is a key feature contributing to their high molecular density and adsorptive capacity for antigens. The PDF explains that this dendrimer-like nature, derived from a genetic variant of sweet corn lacking a debranching enzyme, allows for functionalization of the surface, enabling the positive surface charge of Nano-11 to electrostatically adsorb negatively charged protein antigens. This structure is crucial for their function as effective vaccine adjuvants, facilitating efficient uptake by dendritic cells and subsequent immune activation.